Creating a 2D Fortune's Voronoi diagram with boundary clipping in C# involves several steps. Below is a simplified outline of how you can implement this, along with some example code. This example assumes you have a basic understanding of computational geometry and C# programming.

### Step 1: Set Up Your Project
1. Create a new C# project (e.g., a Console Application or a Windows Forms Application).
2. Add necessary references if you're using a GUI framework.

### Step 2: Define Basic Structures
You will need to define some basic structures for points, edges, and the Voronoi diagram.

```csharp
public struct Point
{
    public double X;
    public double Y;

    public Point(double x, double y)
    {
        X = x;
        Y = y;
    }
}

public struct Edge
{
    public Point Start;
    public Point End;

    public Edge(Point start, Point end)
    {
        Start = start;
        End = end;
    }
}
```

### Step 3: Implement Fortune's Algorithm
Fortune's algorithm is a sweep line algorithm that constructs the Voronoi diagram. Below is a simplified version of the algorithm. Note that this is a complex algorithm, and a full implementation would require handling events, beach lines, and more.

```csharp
public class Voronoi
{
    private List<Point> sites;
    private List<Edge> edges;

    public Voronoi(List<Point> sites)
    {
        this.sites = sites;
        this.edges = new List<Edge>();
    }

    public void GenerateVoronoi()
    {
        // Implement Fortune's algorithm here
        // This is a placeholder for the actual implementation
        // You will need to handle the beach line, events, and edge creation
    }

    public List<Edge> GetEdges()
    {
        return edges;
    }
}
```

### Step 4: Boundary Clipping
To clip the edges of the Voronoi diagram to a specified boundary, you can use the Liang-Barsky algorithm or the Sutherland-Hodgman algorithm. Below is a simple implementation of the Liang-Barsky algorithm for line clipping.

```csharp
public static bool ClipLine(ref Point p0, ref Point p1, double xMin, double xMax, double yMin, double yMax)
{
    double dx = p1.X - p0.X;
    double dy = p1.Y - p0.Y;

    double t0 = 0.0;
    double t1 = 1.0;
    double p, q, r;

    // Clip against the left boundary
    p = -dx; q = p0.X - xMin;
    if (ClipTest(p, q, ref t0, ref t1) == false) return false;

    // Clip against the right boundary
    p = dx; q = xMax - p0.X;
    if (ClipTest(p, q, ref t0, ref t1) == false) return false;

    // Clip against the bottom boundary
    p = -dy; q = p0.Y - yMin;
    if (ClipTest(p, q, ref t0, ref t1) == false) return false;

    // Clip against the top boundary
    p = dy; q = yMax - p0.Y;
    if (ClipTest(p, q, ref t0, ref t1) == false) return false;

    // Now we have a valid line segment
    if (t1 < 1.0)
    {
        p1.X = p0.X + t1 * dx;
        p1.Y = p0.Y + t1 * dy;
    }
    if (t0 > 0.0)
    {
        p0.X = p0.X + t0 * dx;
        p0.Y = p0.Y + t0 * dy;
    }

    return true;
}

private static bool ClipTest(double p, double q, ref double t0, ref double t1)
{
    double r;
    if (p < 0.0)
    {
        r = q / p;
        if (r > t1) return false;
        if (r > t0) t0 = r;
    }
    else if (p > 0.0)
    {
        r = q / p;
        if (r < t0) return false;
        if (r < t1) t1 = r;
    }
    else if (q < 0.0) return false;

    return true;
}
```

### Step 5: Putting It All Together
You can now create a method to generate the Voronoi diagram, clip the edges, and render them. If you're using a GUI framework, you can draw the edges on a canvas.

```csharp
public void DrawVoronoi(Graphics g, double xMin, double xMax, double yMin, double yMax)
{
    foreach (var edge in edges)
    {
        Point p0 = edge.Start;
        Point p1 = edge.End;

        if (ClipLine(ref p0, ref p1, xMin, xMax, yMin, yMax))
        {
            g.DrawLine(Pens.Black, (float)p0.X, (float)p0.Y, (float)p1.X, (float)p1.Y);
        }
    }
}
```

### Step 6: Testing
You can test your implementation by creating a list of points and calling the `GenerateVoronoi` method, followed by the `DrawVoronoi` method in your main application loop.

### Note
This is a simplified version of the Voronoi diagram generation and clipping process. A complete implementation would require handling various edge cases, managing events, and ensuring the Voronoi diagram is correctly constructed. You may also want to consider using existing libraries for computational geometry if you need a more robust solution.

