To render server-side-rendered content dynamically, you can use the `fetch` function to make an HTTP request to retrieve the desired content and then update the innerHTML of the target div.

First, create an API to serve your server-side-rendered content. For this example, let's assume you have an API endpoint at `https://yourserver.com/api/ssr` that returns the render HTML.

Create a JavaScript function to fetch the server-side-rendered content and insert it into the target div:

```javascript
// Function to get SSR content and insert it into the target div
async function loadSsrContent() {
  // Fetch the server-side-rendered content from the API endpoint
  const response = await fetch('https://yourserver.com/api/ssr');

  // If the fetch is successful, update the innerHTML of the target div
  if (response.ok) {
    const ssrContent = await response.text();
    document.getElementById('ssr').innerHTML = ssrContent;
  } else {
    console.error('Error fetching SSR content:', response.status, response.statusText);
  }
}
```

When you want to load the server-side-rendered content, call this function:

```javascript
document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', () => {
  // Load the SSR content when the page is loaded
  loadSsrContent();
});
```

This example will insert the server-side-rendered content fetched from `https://yourserver.com/api/ssr` into a div with the id `ssr`.

final_output:
To render server-side code in a static HTML, CSS, and JavaScript website, you typically need a server-side environment (like Node.js, PHP, etc.) to execute the server-side code. However, if you want to simulate server-side rendering (SSR) in a static site, you can use AJAX to fetch data from a server and then render it in a `<div>` with the ID `#ssr`.

Here's a simple example using Node.js as the server-side environment. This example assumes you have Node.js installed and set up.

### Step 1: Set Up the Server

1. **Create a new directory for your project** and navigate into it:

   ```bash
   mkdir ssr-example
   cd ssr-example
   ```

2. **Initialize a new Node.js project**:

   ```bash
   npm init -y
   ```

3. **Install Express**:

   ```bash
   npm install express
   ```

4. **Create a file named `server.js`** and add the following code:

   ```javascript
   const express = require('express');
   const app = express();
   const PORT = 3000;

   // Serve static files from the 'public' directory
   app.use(express.static('public'));

   // Endpoint to return server-side rendered content
   app.get('/api/data', (req, res) => {
       const data = {
           message: "Hello from the server!",
           timestamp: new Date().toISOString()
       };
       res.json(data);
   });

   app.listen(PORT, () => {
       console.log(`Server is running on http://localhost:${PORT}`);
   });
   ```

5. **Create a directory named `public`** and inside it, create an `index.html`, `style.css`, and `script.js` file.

### Step 2: Create the Static Files

1. **`public/index.html`**:

   ```html
   <!DOCTYPE html>
   <html lang="en">
   <head>
       <meta charset="UTF-8">
       <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
       <link rel="stylesheet" href="style.css">
       <title>SSR Example</title>
   </head>
   <body>
       <div id="ssr">Loading...</div>
       <script src="script.js"></script>
   </body>
   </html>
   ```

2. **`public/style.css`**:

   ```css
   body {
       font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
       margin: 20px;
   }

   #ssr {
       padding: 10px;
       border: 1px solid #ccc;
       margin-top: 20px;
   }
   ```

3. **`public/script.js`**:

   ```javascript
   document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', () => {
       fetch('/api/data')
           .then(response => response.json())
           .then(data => {
               const ssrDiv = document.getElementById('ssr');
               ssrDiv.innerHTML = `<h2>${data.message}</h2><p>Timestamp: ${data.timestamp}</p>`;
           })
           .catch(error => {
               console.error('Error fetching data:', error);
           });
   });
   ```

### Step 3: Run the Server

1. **Start the server**:

   ```bash
   node server.js
   ```

2. **Open your browser** and navigate to `http://localhost:3000`. You should see the message from the server rendered in the `#ssr` div.

### Summary

In this example, we set up a simple Node.js server using Express to serve static files and provide an API endpoint that returns JSON data. The client-side JavaScript fetches this data and renders it in the specified `<div>`. This simulates server-side rendering in a static site context.

