Yes, D3D11 has an interface to set the line width before drawing a line. The interface is called ID3D11DeviceContext and specifically, the Draw method with primitive topology set to D3D11_PRIMITIVE_TOPOLOGY_LINELIST or D3D11_PRIMITIVE_TOPOLOGY_LINESTRIP.

To set the line width, you need to create a rasterizer state object with the desired line width value and set it as the current rasterizer state before calling the Draw method. Here is an example of how to create a rasterizer state with a line width of 2.0:

```C++
D3D11_RASTERIZER_DESC rasterizerDesc = {};
rasterizerDesc.FillMode = D3D11_FILL_SOLID;
rasterizerDesc.CullMode = D3D11_CULL_NONE;
rasterizerDesc.DepthClipEnable = true;
rasterizerDesc.AntialiasedLineEnable = true;
rasterizerDesc.MultisampleEnable = true;
rasterizerDesc.ScissorEnable = false;
rasterizerDesc.SlopeScaledDepthBias = 0.0f;
rasterizerDesc.DepthBias = 0;

rasterizerDesc.FillMode = D3D11_FILL_SOLID;

// Set line width to 2.0f
rasterizerDesc.AntialiasedLineEnable = TRUE;
rasterizerDesc.MultisampleEnable = TRUE;
rasterizerDesc.DepthClipEnable = TRUE;
rasterizerDesc.CullMode = D3D11_CULL_BACK;

rasterizerDesc.FrontCounterClockwise = false;
rasterizerDesc.ScissorEnable = false;
rasterizerDesc.SlopeScaledDepthBias = 0.0f;
rasterizerDesc.DepthBias = 0;

ID3D11RasterizerState* rasterizerState;
device->CreateRasterizerState(&rasterizerDesc, &rasterizerState);

// Set the rasterizer state with the desired line width value
deviceContext->RSSetState(rasterizerState);

// Draw the line(s)
deviceContext->Draw(lineCount, vertexOffset);
```

Here are some resources for further reading on this topic:

- MSDN documentation on ID3D11DeviceContext: https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/d3d11/nn-d3d11-id3d11devicecontext

- MSDN documentation on D3D11_PRIMITIVE_TOPOLOGY: https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/d3dcommon/ne-d3dcommon-d3d11_primitive_topology

- MSDN documentation on ID3D11RasterizerState: https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/d3d11/ns-d3d11-d3d11_rasterizer_desc

- An example of how to use ID3D11RasterizerState to set the line width in DirectX11: https://www.3dgep.com/directx-11-tutorial/#Rasterizer_State_Object

I hope this helps! Let me know if you have any further questions.

final_output:
In Direct3D 11 (D3D11), there is no direct way to set the line width for drawing lines as you would in some other graphics APIs. D3D11 primarily uses triangles for rendering, and the concept of line width is not natively supported in the same way as it is in OpenGL.

However, you can achieve the effect of varying line widths by using geometry shaders or by rendering textured quads that represent lines. Here are a couple of approaches you can take:

1. **Using Geometry Shaders**: You can create a geometry shader that takes a line as input and expands it into a quad with the desired width. This allows you to control the width of the lines dynamically.

2. **Rendering Quads**: Instead of drawing lines, you can draw quads (two triangles) that represent the lines. You would calculate the vertices of the quads based on the desired line width.

### Resources

Here are some resources that can help you understand how to implement these techniques:

1. **Geometry Shader Example**:
   - [Direct3D 11 Geometry Shader Tutorial](https://www.rastertek.com/dx11tut12.html) - This tutorial covers how to create and use geometry shaders in Direct3D 11.

2. **Rendering Lines with Quads**:
   - [Direct3D 11 Line Rendering](https://www.rastertek.com/dx11tut10.html) - This tutorial discusses rendering lines using triangles, which can be adapted to create quads for line rendering.

3. **DirectX SDK Documentation**:
   - [Direct3D 11 Documentation](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/_direct3d) - The official Microsoft documentation for Direct3D 11, which includes information on shaders and rendering techniques.

### Example of Using Geometry Shader for Line Width

Here’s a simple outline of how you might implement a geometry shader to control line width:

1. **Vertex Shader**: Pass the vertex positions to the geometry shader.
2. **Geometry Shader**: In the geometry shader, take the line vertices and create quads based on the desired width.
3. **Pixel Shader**: Render the quads as you would normally.

### Sample Code Snippet

Here’s a very basic example of what the geometry shader might look like:

```hlsl
[maxvertexcount(4)]
void GS_LineWidth(triangle VS_OUTPUT input[3], inout TriangleStream<PS_INPUT> triStream)
{
    // Calculate the direction of the line
    float2 dir = normalize(input[1].position.xy - input[0].position.xy);
    float2 normal = float2(-dir.y, dir.x) * lineWidth; // lineWidth is a float parameter

    // Create the quad vertices
    PS_INPUT output[4];
    output[0].position = input[0].position + float4(normal, 0, 0);
    output[1].position = input[0].position - float4(normal, 0, 0);
    output[2].position = input[1].position + float4(normal, 0, 0);
    output[3].position = input[1].position - float4(normal, 0, 0);

    // Emit the quads
    for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++)
    {
        triStream.Append(output[i]);
    }
    triStream.RestartStrip();
}
```

This is a simplified example, and you would need to handle additional details such as input layout and pixel shading.

### Conclusion

While D3D11 does not provide a direct interface for setting line width, you can achieve the desired effect through geometry shaders or by rendering quads. The resources provided should help you get started with these techniques.

