Yes, you can use Firestore to fetch data and implement clean architecture. Here's a basic example of how you can set up the Firestore functionality and use ViewModel to manage your data.

1. First, add the Firebase and Firestore dependencies to your project's build.gradle file:

```groovy
dependencies {
  implementation 'com.google.firebase:firebase-core:19.0.1'
  implementation 'com.google.firebase:firebase-firestore:24.0.1'
}
```

2. Create a `TimePeriod` enum to represent the different time periods:

```kotlin
enum class TimePeriod {
    WEEKLY,
    MONTHLY,
    YEARLY
}
```

3. Create a `Report` data class to represent your report data:

```kotlin
data class Report(
    val title: String,
    val data: Map<String, Any>
)
```

4. Create a `ReportRepository` class to fetch the data from Firestore:

```kotlin
class ReportRepository {

    private val db = FirebaseFirestore.getInstance()

    fun getReports(timePeriod: TimePeriod, callback: (List<Report>) -> Unit) {
        db.collection(timePeriod.toString().toLowerCase()).get()
            .addOnSuccessListener { querySnapshot ->
                val reports = querySnapshot.map {
                    val title = it.getString("title") ?: ""
                    val data = it.data as? Map<String, Any> ?: emptyMap()
                    Report(title, data)
                }
                callback(reports)
            }
            .addOnFailureListener {
                callback(emptyList())
            }
    }
}
```

5. Create a `ReportViewModel` class to handle the data using ViewModel:

```kotlin
class ReportViewModel(private val reportRepository: ReportRepository) : ViewModel() {

    private val _reports = MutableLiveData<List<Report>>()
    val reports: LiveData<List<Report>>
        get() = _reports

    fun fetchReports(timePeriod: TimePeriod) {
        reportRepository.getReports(timePeriod) { fetchedReports ->
            _reports.value = fetchedReports
        }
    }
}
```

6. Finally, use the `ReportViewModel` in your activity or fragment to fetch the data and observe changes:

```kotlin
class MainActivity : AppCompatActivity() {

    private lateinit var reportViewModel: ReportViewModel

    override fun onCreate(savedInstanceState: Bundle?) {
        super.onCreate(savedInstanceState)
        setContentView(R.layout.activity_main)

        reportViewModel = ViewModelProvider(this).get(ReportViewModel::class.java)

        reportViewModel.reports.observe(this, Observer { reports ->
            // Update your UI with the fetched reports
        })

        // Fetch reports for a specific time period
        reportViewModel.fetchReports(TimePeriod.MONTHLY)
    }
}
```

This is a basic example of how to use Firestore to fetch data for a specific time period and utilize ViewModel for handling data in an Android app. You might need to adjust the data class and Firestore structure based on your actual data requirements.

final_output:
Certainly! To implement Firestore functionality in an Android application using a clean architecture approach with ViewModel, you can follow these steps. Below is a simplified example that demonstrates how to fetch data from Firestore for weekly, monthly, and yearly intervals using a ViewModel.

### Step 1: Set Up Firestore

Make sure you have added Firestore to your Android project. In your `build.gradle` (app level), add the following dependencies:

```groovy
implementation 'com.google.firebase:firebase-firestore-ktx:24.0.0' // Check for the latest version
```

### Step 2: Create a Data Model

Create a data model that represents the data you are fetching from Firestore. For example:

```kotlin
data class DataModel(
    val id: String = "",
    val value: Int = 0,
    val timestamp: Long = 0L
)
```

### Step 3: Create a Repository

Create a repository class that will handle the Firestore operations.

```kotlin
class DataRepository {

    private val firestore = FirebaseFirestore.getInstance()

    fun fetchWeeklyData(callback: (List<DataModel>) -> Unit) {
        val startOfWeek = System.currentTimeMillis() - (7 * 24 * 60 * 60 * 1000) // 7 days ago
        firestore.collection("your_collection")
            .whereGreaterThan("timestamp", startOfWeek)
            .get()
            .addOnSuccessListener { documents ->
                val dataList = documents.map { doc ->
                    DataModel(
                        id = doc.id,
                        value = doc.getLong("value")?.toInt() ?: 0,
                        timestamp = doc.getLong("timestamp") ?: 0L
                    )
                }
                callback(dataList)
            }
            .addOnFailureListener { exception ->
                // Handle the error
            }
    }

    fun fetchMonthlyData(callback: (List<DataModel>) -> Unit) {
        val startOfMonth = System.currentTimeMillis() - (30 * 24 * 60 * 60 * 1000) // 30 days ago
        firestore.collection("your_collection")
            .whereGreaterThan("timestamp", startOfMonth)
            .get()
            .addOnSuccessListener { documents ->
                val dataList = documents.map { doc ->
                    DataModel(
                        id = doc.id,
                        value = doc.getLong("value")?.toInt() ?: 0,
                        timestamp = doc.getLong("timestamp") ?: 0L
                    )
                }
                callback(dataList)
            }
            .addOnFailureListener { exception ->
                // Handle the error
            }
    }

    fun fetchYearlyData(callback: (List<DataModel>) -> Unit) {
        val startOfYear = System.currentTimeMillis() - (365 * 24 * 60 * 60 * 1000) // 365 days ago
        firestore.collection("your_collection")
            .whereGreaterThan("timestamp", startOfYear)
            .get()
            .addOnSuccessListener { documents ->
                val dataList = documents.map { doc ->
                    DataModel(
                        id = doc.id,
                        value = doc.getLong("value")?.toInt() ?: 0,
                        timestamp = doc.getLong("timestamp") ?: 0L
                    )
                }
                callback(dataList)
            }
            .addOnFailureListener { exception ->
                // Handle the error
            }
    }
}
```

### Step 4: Create a ViewModel

Create a ViewModel that will interact with the repository and provide data to the UI.

```kotlin
class DataViewModel : ViewModel() {

    private val repository = DataRepository()
    private val _weeklyData = MutableLiveData<List<DataModel>>()
    val weeklyData: LiveData<List<DataModel>> get() = _weeklyData

    private val _monthlyData = MutableLiveData<List<DataModel>>()
    val monthlyData: LiveData<List<DataModel>> get() = _monthlyData

    private val _yearlyData = MutableLiveData<List<DataModel>>()
    val yearlyData: LiveData<List<DataModel>> get() = _yearlyData

    fun fetchWeeklyData() {
        repository.fetchWeeklyData { data ->
            _weeklyData.postValue(data)
        }
    }

    fun fetchMonthlyData() {
        repository.fetchMonthlyData { data ->
            _monthlyData.postValue(data)
        }
    }

    fun fetchYearlyData() {
        repository.fetchYearlyData { data ->
            _yearlyData.postValue(data)
        }
    }
}
```

### Step 5: Use ViewModel in Your Activity/Fragment

In your Activity or Fragment, observe the LiveData from the ViewModel and update the UI accordingly.

```kotlin
class YourActivity : AppCompatActivity() {

    private lateinit var viewModel: DataViewModel

    override fun onCreate(savedInstanceState: Bundle?) {
        super.onCreate(savedInstanceState)
        setContentView(R.layout.activity_your)

        viewModel = ViewModelProvider(this).get(DataViewModel::class.java)

        viewModel.weeklyData.observe(this, { data ->
            // Update your UI with weekly data
        })

        viewModel.monthlyData.observe(this, { data ->
            // Update your UI with monthly data
        })

        viewModel.yearlyData.observe(this, { data ->
            // Update your UI with yearly data
        })

        // Fetch data
        viewModel.fetchWeeklyData()
        viewModel.fetchMonthlyData()
        viewModel.fetchYearlyData()
    }
}
```

### Conclusion

This is a basic implementation of fetching data from Firestore using a clean architecture approach with ViewModel. You can expand upon this by adding error handling, loading states, and more complex data processing as needed. Make sure to replace `"your_collection"` with the actual name of your Firestore collection.

