Creating a simple HTML website that resembles a database can be done using basic HTML, CSS, and a bit of JavaScript. Below is an example of a simple website that displays a list of items (like a database) and allows users to add new items to the list. This example does not use a real database but simulates one using JavaScript arrays.

### HTML Structure

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Simple Database Simulation</title>
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="styles.css">
</head>
<body>
    <div class="container">
        <h1>Simple Database Simulation</h1>
        <div class="form-container">
            <input type="text" id="itemInput" placeholder="Enter item name">
            <button id="addItemButton">Add Item</button>
        </div>
        <h2>Items List</h2>
        <ul id="itemsList"></ul>
    </div>

    <script src="script.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
```

### CSS (styles.css)

```css
body {
    font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
    background-color: #f4f4f4;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 20px;
}

.container {
    max-width: 600px;
    margin: auto;
    background: white;
    padding: 20px;
    border-radius: 5px;
    box-shadow: 0 0 10px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1);
}

h1, h2 {
    color: #333;
}

.form-container {
    margin-bottom: 20px;
}

input[type="text"] {
    width: 70%;
    padding: 10px;
    border: 1px solid #ccc;
    border-radius: 5px;
}

button {
    padding: 10px 15px;
    background-color: #28a745;
    color: white;
    border: none;
    border-radius: 5px;
    cursor: pointer;
}

button:hover {
    background-color: #218838;
}

ul {
    list-style-type: none;
    padding: 0;
}

li {
    padding: 10px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid #ccc;
}
```

### JavaScript (script.js)

```javascript
document.getElementById('addItemButton').addEventListener('click', function() {
    const itemInput = document.getElementById('itemInput');
    const itemName = itemInput.value.trim();

    if (itemName) {
        addItemToList(itemName);
        itemInput.value = ''; // Clear the input field
    } else {
        alert('Please enter an item name.');
    }
});

function addItemToList(itemName) {
    const itemsList = document.getElementById('itemsList');
    const listItem = document.createElement('li');
    listItem.textContent = itemName;
    itemsList.appendChild(listItem);
}
```

### How to Use

1. **Create the Files**: Create three files named `index.html`, `styles.css`, and `script.js`.
2. **Copy the Code**: Copy the respective code snippets into each file.
3. **Open in Browser**: Open `index.html` in a web browser to see the simple database simulation in action.

### Functionality

- Users can enter an item name in the input field and click the "Add Item" button.
- The item will be added to the list below, simulating a database of items.
- The input field will clear after adding an item.

This is a basic example and can be expanded with more features like editing or deleting items, or even integrating with a real backend database using technologies like Node.js, PHP, or Python with a database like MySQL or MongoDB.

